  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1227 AM CDT Wed Oct 7 2026\n\nToday - Friday: Mostly Dry with Near to Slightly Above Normal \nTemperatures\n\nNorthwesterly flow continues to dominate the synoptic flow pattern \nthrough the end of the week which in turn will keep temperatures \nlargely steady-state with highs in the middle 60s to lower 70s. \nAdditionally, with minimal synoptic forcing really not expecting \nmuch for precipitation. If there is any chance for precipitation, it \nwould likely be on Friday morning as a stripe of 850mb warm air \nadvection pushes north into the region on the western periphery of a \nsurface high that is pushing eastward. Overall, the grand ensemble \n(GEFS/EC/Canadian ensemble) has some measurable probabilities (20-\n60%) with the highest probabilities across northeast IA where this \nforcing regime is the strongest. However, given that the available \nmoisture is not overly respectable with this forcing, would think \nthat precipitation amounts will be limited. \n\nThis Weekend - Early Next Week: Weekend Warm Up, Precipitation \nPotential Early Next Week\n\nAs we head into the weekend, northwesterly flow turns to zonal as a \nbroad upper-level ridge moves through the region. Consequently, the \nlow-level winds exhibit a notable response with a tightening surface \npressure gradient on Saturday allowing for breezy southerly winds on \nSaturday afternoon. Given fairly decent diurnal mixing in the \nafternoon, noting some probabilities (30-60%) in the NBM on Saturday \nfor wind gusts over 35 mph, primarily across southeast MN. \nOtherwise, would expect temperatures to warm in a fairly pronounced \nfashion with some probabilities (10-40%) for highs to top 80 degrees \nin the grand ensemble (GEFS/EC/Canadian ensemble) across the local \narea on Saturday and Sunday. Thinking that given the setup that \nthese probabilities may be a bit higher due to the southerly flow \nand ridge overhead. \n\nAs we head into early next week, precipitation chances will begin to \nincrease as a trough situated over the Rockies begins to be \ntranslated eastbound which in turn will flatten the aforementioned \nridge that is present over the weekend. Consequently, measurable \nprecipitation probabilities in the grand ensemble (GEFS/EC/Canadian \nensemble) begin to increase (30-50%) over the local area beginning \non Monday. However, this is widely dependent on how quickly that \ntrough over the Rockies highlighted in much of deterministic \nguidance begins to slide eastward. If this trough is slower to push \neastward and leaves the ridge in place, we could even some moisture \nfrom the remnant tropical cyclone moving northward from the Deep \nSouth into early next week across southern portions of our local \narea. However, this would be a fairly low confidence scenario. In \nany case, the general gist of the upcoming week would suggest \nincreasing precipitation potential.",
